WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:10.353
The armed conflicts that marked the 20th and early 21st centuries all have a root,

00:00:10.614 --> 00:00:11.134
even tenuous,

00:00:11.573 --> 00:00:14.054
in the treaties and the fragile peace of 1919.

00:00:20.557 --> 00:00:21.617
They are like skipping stones,

00:00:22.077 --> 00:00:22.798
the convulsions,

00:00:23.097 --> 00:00:25.018
The epilogues of a century-old story.

00:00:37.948 --> 00:00:40.069
Why is the Middle East today?

00:00:40.209 --> 00:00:43.570
Are Sarajevo still places of tension and conflict?

00:00:43.871 --> 00:00:44.231
All of that,

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:49.633
It evokes events and tensions that took place during the First World War.

00:00:50.012 --> 00:00:54.034
When we look at all the margins of the current European Union,

00:00:54.135 --> 00:01:04.519
We realize that they correspond to all those areas of fracture and rupture in which treaties failed to provide positive solutions at the end of the First World War.

00:01:09.763 --> 00:01:10.263
Sarajevo,

00:01:10.964 --> 00:01:11.864
Bosnia and Herzegovina.

00:01:12.464 --> 00:01:14.405
A commercial crossroads since time immemorial,

00:01:14.906 --> 00:01:17.267
a cosmopolitan city where religions live together.

00:01:17.907 --> 00:01:19.128
This is the Jerusalem of the Balkans,

00:01:19.368 --> 00:01:20.469
where over less than 500 meters,

00:01:20.649 --> 00:01:21.929
The mosque is next to the synagogue.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.210
the Orthodox Church,

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:24.250
the cathedral.

00:01:24.991 --> 00:01:26.532
It all started in this small town.

00:01:27.173 --> 00:01:29.433
Here, on June 28, 1914,

00:01:30.013 --> 00:01:31.354
a Serbian student from Bosnia,

00:01:31.794 --> 00:01:32.536
Gavrilo Princip,

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:37.881
unwittingly triggered a global catastrophe by assassinating Archduke Franz Ferdinand.

00:01:38.242 --> 00:01:39.962
heir to the throne of the Austro-Hungarian Empire.

00:01:40.762 --> 00:01:41.703
It is 11 a.m.

00:01:42.243 --> 00:01:43.743
when, after a first failed attack,

00:01:45.024 --> 00:01:46.464
Principe is located on the boulevard,

00:01:46.944 --> 00:01:47.724
just around the corner.

00:01:51.165 --> 00:01:52.086
The car stops there.

00:01:52.506 --> 00:01:59.108
As fate would have it, François Ferdinand arrived at the feet of the man who would kill him a few seconds later.

00:01:59.548 --> 00:02:03.831
Principe was a young nationalist who dreamed of the creation of a unified Yugoslavia.

00:02:04.131 --> 00:02:04.391
For him,

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:05.591
François Ferdinand,

00:02:05.691 --> 00:02:07.013
to embody the empire,

00:02:07.153 --> 00:02:07.533
evil,

00:02:07.932 --> 00:02:08.674
the occupation,

00:02:08.954 --> 00:02:09.114
All,

00:02:09.574 --> 00:02:15.877
It is simply the act of someone who dreamed of freedom, Yugoslavia, and justice.

00:02:16.158 --> 00:02:18.259
To pull meant to free oneself.

00:02:25.072 --> 00:02:28.673
Sarajevo was in a way the triggering event of the First World War.

00:02:29.314 --> 00:02:33.635
In fact, the symbolic starting point with this assassination had consequences throughout the world.

00:02:34.175 --> 00:02:42.057
It is the result of a very local context and the emergence of nationalism or the beginning of a national sentiment of the struggle for the creation of a new state.

00:02:44.778 --> 00:02:46.679
This small, local winter event led to,

00:02:47.019 --> 00:02:48.099
through the system of alliances,

00:02:48.439 --> 00:02:49.640
one of the world's greatest conflicts.

00:02:51.072 --> 00:02:51.832
1917.

00:02:52.753 --> 00:02:54.754
After three years of pointless slaughter and assaults,

00:02:55.114 --> 00:02:56.294
The Americans go to war.

00:02:57.855 --> 00:02:58.534
A game changer,

00:02:58.995 --> 00:03:00.556
and give a second wind to the allied troops.

00:03:01.856 --> 00:03:02.977
For an American who is fighting,

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:04.878
six men in the rear in logistics.

00:03:05.338 --> 00:03:06.117
200,000 per day.

00:03:08.058 --> 00:03:10.900
The advantage was quickly regained against the Germans, who failed to retake Paris.

00:03:14.022 --> 00:03:15.722
At the end of the summer of 1918,

00:03:16.163 --> 00:03:19.243
the latest German offensives on the French front

00:03:19.843 --> 00:03:20.685
They all failed.

00:03:20.944 --> 00:03:28.191
Foch launched a general offensive across the entire Allied front, pushing the Germans back towards their borders.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.711
towards the borders of Belgium,

00:03:30.073 --> 00:03:35.296
by forcing them to evacuate all the regions that have been occupied since the fall

00:03:37.759 --> 00:03:40.480
1914. At the end of 1918,

00:03:40.961 --> 00:03:42.122
The German army is exhausted.

00:03:44.304 --> 00:03:46.605
Everyone in the army knows that you can't win the war.

00:03:49.761 --> 00:03:51.221
We know it's not going to go well.

00:03:53.582 --> 00:03:56.025
But no one can say how long the front will hold.

00:03:59.766 --> 00:04:01.568
And from the summer of 1918,

00:04:02.289 --> 00:04:04.570
The hope of victory no longer resonates with the population.

00:04:06.572 --> 00:04:08.433
We continue because we have no other choice.

00:04:09.354 --> 00:04:10.493
But there is hunger in the homes.

00:04:10.895 --> 00:04:12.296
We no longer understand this war.

00:04:12.796 --> 00:04:13.957
We don't want this war anymore.

00:04:15.518 --> 00:04:18.420
German soldiers surrender by the thousands and mutinies break out.

00:04:20.973 --> 00:04:22.132
From that moment on,

00:04:22.834 --> 00:04:27.255
The soldiers will simply return home and no one will forcibly bring them back to the front.

00:04:27.735 --> 00:04:29.677
because the command structure is no longer working very well.

00:04:32.879 --> 00:04:39.403
And it is in this atmosphere that sailors' strikes will break out in Wilhelmshaven and Kiel.

00:04:41.644 --> 00:04:42.744
And in two or three days,

00:04:43.264 --> 00:04:45.846
This movement is growing so large that 30,000 men,

00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:49.127
as many as 40,000 men will demonstrate in the streets chanting "Thank you".

00:04:49.467 --> 00:04:50.889
The war is over.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:52.751
On the Allied side,

00:04:53.271 --> 00:04:54.693
This is the moment of the decisive battle.

00:04:55.435 --> 00:04:59.559
There is no breakthrough in the strict sense of the Hindenburg Line.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:03.360
The Germans' last line of defense, or defense system,

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:18.665
There is an almost arithmetic pressure exerted on the entire front, and it is the power of the Allied economy and industry that is forcing the German army to retreat and evacuate all the territories it occupied.

00:05:20.305 --> 00:05:22.966
With, for the first time, the use of a new weapon.

00:05:24.606 --> 00:05:26.367
The tank-aircraft pairing,

00:05:26.447 --> 00:05:28.848
Pétain used it for the first time.

00:05:29.195 --> 00:05:33.718
during the month of July 1918 to stop the last German offensives.

00:05:36.459 --> 00:05:37.000
In Germany,

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:38.721
The situation is catastrophic.

00:05:39.301 --> 00:05:41.242
The allies have imposed a food blockade.

00:05:41.923 --> 00:05:42.603
For civilians,

00:05:43.144 --> 00:05:46.925
It's already unbearable when hunger is added to a difficult daily life.

00:05:49.847 --> 00:05:51.228
The children have nothing left to eat.

00:05:52.209 --> 00:05:56.411
The women send letters to their husbands at the front asking them to send food to Germany.

00:05:59.237 --> 00:06:00.278
And that's what men did.

00:06:03.021 --> 00:06:11.687
And it is very clear that the brutality of the German occupiers in northern France and the mass requisitions were commensurate with what they lacked at home.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:16.071
Strikes break out every day.

00:06:16.631 --> 00:06:17.372
Throughout Germany,

00:06:17.632 --> 00:06:20.014
Bolshevik ideas are gaining ground and shaking the Empire.

00:06:21.074 --> 00:06:22.876
The Red Revolution is on the verge of succeeding.

00:06:23.663 --> 00:06:24.383
The beginning of November,

00:06:24.424 --> 00:06:25.784
There is this revolution in Germany,

00:06:26.245 --> 00:06:32.128
And of course we fear that it will be the same scenario as in Russia.

00:06:32.548 --> 00:06:32.948
SO,

00:06:33.468 --> 00:06:34.629
We were fed up with the ending.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:36.130
and with the end,

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:37.891
We were fed up with the war.

00:06:39.733 --> 00:06:41.133
German public opinion has had enough.

00:06:41.693 --> 00:06:43.294
The general staff must face the facts.

00:06:43.774 --> 00:06:44.915
We must request an armistice.

00:06:47.056 --> 00:06:48.436
Hindenburg and Ludendorff,

00:06:48.517 --> 00:06:50.438
the two leaders of the German army,

00:06:50.918 --> 00:06:51.498
they say,

00:06:51.938 --> 00:06:52.839
We are lost.

00:06:53.408 --> 00:06:55.909
We are retreating on the French front.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:58.391
Our safety is no longer guaranteed.

00:06:58.913 --> 00:07:02.355
We need to get out of this war as quickly as possible.

00:07:03.836 --> 00:07:05.459
From October 1918,

00:07:05.459 --> 00:07:06.839
Talks begin with Wilson,

00:07:07.039 --> 00:07:08.240
the President of the United States,

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:09.862
who has a big project in mind.

00:07:10.442 --> 00:07:11.223
To propose peace,

00:07:11.543 --> 00:07:11.923
just,

00:07:12.245 --> 00:07:15.367
based on 14 points that will revolutionize future international relations.

00:07:16.507 --> 00:07:18.850
The Germans see this as an opportunity for an honorable end to the conflict.

00:07:20.672 --> 00:07:22.233
Starting in September-October,

00:07:22.453 --> 00:07:23.894
We are talking about Wilson's peace.

00:07:26.636 --> 00:07:35.162
It gives German democrats a tiny bit of hope that they could achieve a good peace and that the new system will lead to a good peace.

00:07:37.424 --> 00:07:37.804
As a result,

00:07:38.385 --> 00:07:39.806
the wish that the war must end,

00:07:40.506 --> 00:07:42.388
that Wilson's peace must come about,

00:07:43.188 --> 00:07:45.009
will become very prominent.

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:45.770
Very quickly,

00:07:45.771 --> 00:07:48.512
The French, the British, and the Italians are furious.

00:07:48.692 --> 00:07:50.634
because they feel we weren't consulted.

00:07:51.213 --> 00:07:52.155
They set their conditions.

00:07:52.175 --> 00:07:53.756
They make Wilson understand,

00:07:53.957 --> 00:07:55.558
Why not use the 14-point system as a base?

00:07:55.838 --> 00:07:57.580
But security still needs to be ensured.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:07:59.001
in particular the security of France.

00:07:59.441 --> 00:08:02.884
The Germans made contact for the armistice via radio.

00:08:03.685 --> 00:08:06.067
The Germans will be allowed,

00:08:06.387 --> 00:08:08.409
politicians and military personnel united,

00:08:08.709 --> 00:08:13.794
to cross French lines and reach a forest,

00:08:13.834 --> 00:08:15.055
Why? To be safe.

00:08:15.995 --> 00:08:16.576
the French.

00:08:19.979 --> 00:08:21.640
And this forest was not chosen by chance.

00:08:22.241 --> 00:08:23.622
It is located near Sanlis,

00:08:24.002 --> 00:08:25.843
where Marshal Foch has his headquarters.

00:08:29.326 --> 00:08:30.046
It's at Rotonde,

00:08:30.608 --> 00:08:31.467
in a clearing,

00:08:31.828 --> 00:08:33.309
near a railway intersection,

00:08:33.829 --> 00:08:35.290
that the ceasefire will be signed.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:47.299
This is the first time Marshal Foch has seen the German plenipotentiaries.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:48.140
At Rotonde,

00:08:48.341 --> 00:08:50.622
This was three days before the signing of the armistice.

00:08:50.623 --> 00:08:51.383
It's November 8th.

00:08:52.003 --> 00:09:00.089
Marshal Foch had the German plenipotentiaries sit in the carriage and asked them innocently what the purpose of their visit was.

00:09:00.589 --> 00:09:04.331
Then the German commissioners got a little confused and said, "Listen,

00:09:04.572 --> 00:09:08.774
We've come to see what the proposals are.

00:09:09.815 --> 00:09:12.257
And the marshal said, "But I have no proposal to make to you."

00:09:12.897 --> 00:09:14.138
So he rephrases their question

00:09:14.618 --> 00:09:16.039
"What are the conditions of the armistice?"

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:16.760
Ah!

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:22.383
You ask for an armistice and he takes the sheet of paper out of his pocket and tells them the conditions of the armistice.

00:09:25.445 --> 00:09:27.606
The atmosphere was fine,

00:09:28.227 --> 00:09:28.667
nothing more.

00:09:28.727 --> 00:09:30.869
In other words, Marshal Foch was dry.

00:09:31.429 --> 00:09:32.389
There's no discussion.

00:09:32.409 --> 00:09:33.790
You have come to ask for an armistice,

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:34.531
Yes or no.

00:09:35.792 --> 00:09:38.753
Until the bells rang at 11 a.m. on the 11th,

00:09:39.614 --> 00:09:42.216
It's an armistice that will be secret.

00:09:46.912 --> 00:09:49.475
Even though the military considers that it has not suffered a defeat,

00:09:49.917 --> 00:09:53.341
These are German civilians who are concluding four years of war and accepting,

00:09:53.402 --> 00:09:54.222
almost surreptitiously,

00:09:54.764 --> 00:09:57.207
armistice conditions under humiliating circumstances.

00:09:58.149 --> 00:09:58.450
What,

00:09:59.051 --> 00:09:59.752
if it was not intentional,

00:10:00.231 --> 00:10:01.472
sows the seeds of revenge.

00:10:03.352 --> 00:10:09.096
The armistice was imposed on the Germans with very harsh military conditions.

00:10:09.696 --> 00:10:12.918
but which correspond to the current situation.

00:10:13.298 --> 00:10:14.899
Very harsh conditions allow,

00:10:15.318 --> 00:10:16.198
From a French perspective,

00:10:16.578 --> 00:10:17.120
Two things.

00:10:17.779 --> 00:10:17.899
A,

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:20.360
if the Germans accept them,

00:10:20.821 --> 00:10:23.962
to put them in a position that no longer allows them to resume the war.

00:10:24.682 --> 00:10:25.744
If they refuse them,

00:10:26.043 --> 00:10:26.364
well...

00:10:26.727 --> 00:10:30.410
The Allied arithmetic machine will continue to crush the German army.

00:10:32.711 --> 00:10:33.092
It's over.

00:10:33.533 --> 00:10:34.873
In the streets of Paris and elsewhere,

00:10:35.153 --> 00:10:36.274
The people rejoice.

00:10:36.634 --> 00:10:38.275
The soldiers are relieved to be alive.

00:10:39.116 --> 00:10:41.578
Belgium and northern France are now nothing but ruins.

00:10:42.278 --> 00:10:44.019
Villages in the East have been wiped off the map.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:47.141
But the victory of the allies is not the victory of peace.

00:10:47.822 --> 00:10:52.505
Europe must be rebuilt on the ruins of three empires, and this must be settled once and for all.

00:10:53.006 --> 00:10:53.326
it is believed,

00:10:53.886 --> 00:10:54.767
the fate of Germany.

00:10:59.579 --> 00:11:00.900
June 28, 1919,

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:02.160
7 months later.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:04.081
It's a beautiful midday in Versailles.

00:11:04.641 --> 00:11:05.121
A little chilly.

00:11:05.923 --> 00:11:07.402
The area around the castle is packed with people.

00:11:08.303 --> 00:11:09.823
Throughout the next 3 hours,

00:11:10.464 --> 00:11:13.105
Everything was carefully chosen to humiliate the Germans again.

00:11:14.045 --> 00:11:14.365
The place?

00:11:15.105 --> 00:11:16.606
Versailles and its Hall of Mirrors,

00:11:16.826 --> 00:11:18.407
a symbol of French diplomacy,

00:11:19.048 --> 00:11:21.728
where the German Empire was born in 1871.

00:11:22.528 --> 00:11:22.949
The date?

00:11:23.669 --> 00:11:25.830
Five years to the day after the Sarajevo assassination,

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:27.530
pretext for the Great War.

00:11:28.231 --> 00:11:28.651
Men?

00:11:29.152 --> 00:11:29.812
The disfigured faces,

00:11:30.353 --> 00:11:32.615
placed along the route of the Germans before going to sign.

00:11:33.536 --> 00:11:34.937
The Hall of Mirrors is crowded.

00:11:35.456 --> 00:11:36.457
But the Germans are not here.

00:11:37.239 --> 00:11:39.600
They arrive through a hidden door, out of sight.

00:11:40.642 --> 00:11:44.826
The German delegation arrived through this door which leads into Madame Victoire's large study.

00:11:45.186 --> 00:11:46.447
And from this piece,

00:11:46.466 --> 00:11:47.608
She then enters the castle.

00:11:47.968 --> 00:11:50.550
She walks through the last rooms of Madame Victoire's apartment,

00:11:50.831 --> 00:11:56.896
crosses the Queen's grand apartment to emerge at the entrance to the Hall of Mirrors.

00:11:57.196 --> 00:11:58.896
This journey is actually a practical one.

00:11:58.898 --> 00:12:04.480
It was absolutely essential to prevent the German delegation from following the same route as the Allied delegations which,

00:12:04.559 --> 00:12:04.840
they,

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:07.280
entered directly through the royal court.

00:12:07.961 --> 00:12:13.403
But it is certain that the German delegation was to be confronted with the full force of French history.

00:12:13.404 --> 00:12:14.004
if I may say so

00:12:14.284 --> 00:12:15.345
in the Hall of Mirrors itself,

00:12:15.404 --> 00:12:20.268
because this is the grand decor of Louis XIV which magnifies the victories of France against Germany.

00:12:26.327 --> 00:12:26.908
3 p.m.

00:12:27.349 --> 00:12:27.829
Clemenceau,

00:12:28.109 --> 00:12:30.531
surrounded by Lloyd George Langlais and the American Wilson,

00:12:30.651 --> 00:12:31.732
takes place around a desk,

00:12:32.172 --> 00:12:34.033
extracted for the occasion from the national furniture collection.

00:12:34.834 --> 00:12:38.657
So this office was actually located at the almost geometric center of the gallery.

00:12:39.357 --> 00:12:40.118
We are getting closer,

00:12:40.138 --> 00:12:41.177
Here we are in the center.

00:12:41.519 --> 00:12:42.239
He was there,

00:12:42.499 --> 00:12:45.341
with an armchair placed with its back to the windows.

00:12:45.561 --> 00:12:53.507
So the person signing was facing powerful allies who were behind that long table which was in front of the mirrors.

00:12:54.086 --> 00:12:54.988
Silence falls.

00:12:57.175 --> 00:12:59.736
We get the Germans to sign first, just to be on the safe side.

00:13:00.437 --> 00:13:02.258
The representatives of the Weimar Republic,

00:13:02.737 --> 00:13:03.379
Muller and Bell,

00:13:03.899 --> 00:13:04.979
Pale and Malay,

00:13:05.298 --> 00:13:05.658
look,

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:07.120
according to the Figaro columnist,

00:13:07.519 --> 00:13:08.660
of two workers dressed in their Sunday best.

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:10.822
The Minister of Foreign Affairs,

00:13:10.841 --> 00:13:12.021
the representative of Germany,

00:13:12.101 --> 00:13:22.045
refused to use one of the pens provided since they were pens that had been donated by veterans' associations, and so he used his own fountain pen.

00:13:22.066 --> 00:13:25.606
Moreover, it was a treaty that no one on the German side wanted to sign.

00:13:25.607 --> 00:13:26.548
We had to find two.

00:13:26.887 --> 00:13:27.527
of ministers,

00:13:27.528 --> 00:13:28.128
of volunteers

00:13:28.129 --> 00:13:28.608
if I may say so

00:13:28.948 --> 00:13:29.728
to come and sign.

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:34.370
The Germans were naturally humiliated from the start.

00:13:36.631 --> 00:13:37.711
The first humiliation,

00:13:38.173 --> 00:13:38.972
And we often forget that,

00:13:40.633 --> 00:13:41.733
It's because they've been forgotten.

00:13:42.073 --> 00:13:43.474
excluded from peace negotiations.

00:13:46.695 --> 00:13:48.516
When on January 18, 1919,

00:13:49.016 --> 00:13:51.897
Discussions to reach Versailles have begun.

00:13:52.557 --> 00:13:53.778
There are 27 nations,

00:13:54.198 --> 00:13:55.500
but no nation defeated.

00:14:00.203 --> 00:14:00.483
And that,

00:14:00.624 --> 00:14:01.985
throughout the history of treaties,

00:14:02.345 --> 00:14:03.547
That had never happened before.

00:14:05.449 --> 00:14:06.370
The Germans are,

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:07.651
We know this from testimonies,

00:14:07.652 --> 00:14:08.472
on the verge of tears.

00:14:08.792 --> 00:14:11.235
And it's an extremely difficult time.

00:14:11.295 --> 00:14:12.176
extremely painful.

00:14:12.776 --> 00:14:16.080
And I don't think they appreciated it very much.

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:19.063
In any case, they took advantage of the setting that was available to them.

00:14:20.176 --> 00:14:24.638
There is also that incredible scene when the German delegation was led to Versailles.

00:14:25.399 --> 00:14:25.619
Before,

